<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Nicaragua</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cocibolca</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> is the largest lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, connected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> by the San Juan River. Before the Spanish conquest the Nahua-speaking Nicarao lived on the isthmus of Rivas between the lake and the Pacific. In colonial and early independent times <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, on its north-western shore, traded through the lake and the river, and the route was long considered for an interoceanic canal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-nicaragua.jpg" alt="Lake Nicaragua" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zach Klein · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_Nicaragua.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>, also called Cocibolca or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, is a freshwater lake of tectonic origin with an area of 8,264 square kilometres. It lies 32.7 metres above sea level and reaches a depth of 26 metres. Nicaraguans know it as Lago Cocibolca or Mar Dulce, literally the Sweet Sea, since agua dulce is Spanish for freshwater. It is slightly smaller than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a> and is the nation's largest source of freshwater.</p><p>The lake receives the intermittent Tipitapa River when Lake Managua stands high, and drains by the San Juan River eastwards to the Caribbean Sea. Together these waters form the largest international drainage basin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>. Easterly winds raise sizeable waves, and the lake has a reputation for periodically powerful storms. It holds the volcanic islands of Ometepe and Zapatera and the Solentiname archipelago.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Because the San Juan River runs to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> on the north-western shore was historically an Atlantic port, although both the city and the lake lie closer to the Pacific. The Pacific can be seen from the mountains of Ometepe, the largest island. The lake has a history of Caribbean pirates, who assaulted Granada on three occasions.</p><p>During the California Gold Rush of the late 1840s, the trip up the San Juan River, through the lake and across a short overland stretch was one of the two preferred routes between the Atlantic and the Pacific, the other crossing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>. Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-canal">Panama Canal</a> was built, a stagecoach line owned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornelius-vanderbilt">Cornelius Vanderbilt</a>'s Accessory Transit Company linked the lake with the Pacific across the low hills of the Isthmus of Rivas.</p><p>Plans for an interoceanic Nicaragua Canal along this route were made, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-canal">Panama Canal</a> was built instead. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bryan-chamorro-treaty">Bryan–Chamorro Treaty</a> of 1916 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> secured all rights to a canal along the route; the United States and Nicaragua rescinded the treaty by mutual agreement in 1970.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Boats remain the only way to reach the Solentiname archipelago. The lake holds sawfish, tarpon and sharks. Scientists first took the sharks for an endemic species, the Lake Nicaragua shark, but in 1961 it was identified with the widespread bull shark. They were presumed to be trapped, until the late 1960s, when it was found that they can leap up the rapids of the San Juan River almost like salmon. Bull sharks tagged in the lake have since been caught in the open ocean, some completing the journey in as little as seven days.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lake Nicaragua” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>As a member state of the federation, Nicaragua was torn by civil wars between the liberal elite of León, the old colonial administrative centre, and the conservative merchants and landowners of Granada on <b>Lake Nicaragua</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nicarao were Nahua-speaking migrants from central Mexico who settled the isthmus of Rivas between <b>Lake Nicaragua</b> and the Pacific, probably in the 12th or 13th century, displacing earlier Chorotega groups.</blockquote><a href="/state/nicarao-chiefdom">Nicarao chiefdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The rivalry between León, seat of liberal lawyers and officials, and Granada, home of conservative merchants trading through <b>Lake Nicaragua</b> and the San Juan River, produced repeated civil wars.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-nicaragua">State of Nicaragua</a></li></ul></section>
